@node MOVE Statement
@section MOVE Statement

The MOVE statement copies a value from a source to one or more destinations. It is the primary mechanism for variable assignment in COBOL and handles automatic type conversion between numeric types, including BINARY, DECIMAL, and DISPLAY formats.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
MOVE source TO destination [destination] ...
MOVE source TO variable [REPLACING FIRST|ALL 
                         LEADING|TRAILING
                         BY replacement] ...
@end example

@table @asis
@item MOVE
Keyword indicating a data movement operation

@item source
The source operand: a literal (numeric or alphanumeric), a variable, or an expression. Can be a constant (5, "HELLO"), a variable name (COUNTER, PLAYER-NAME), or a special literal (SPACES, ZEROS, LOW-VALUES, HIGH-VALUES)

@item TO
Keyword separating source from destination(s)

@item destination
One or more target variables that receive the source value. Multiple destinations receive identical copies of the source

@item REPLACING
Optional keyword for string replacement operations within the MOVE. Must be used when source and destination are alphanumeric strings

@item FIRST|ALL
FIRST replaces only the first occurrence; ALL replaces all occurrences

@item LEADING|TRAILING
LEADING matches at the beginning of the string; TRAILING matches at the end

@item BY
Specifies the replacement string when using REPLACING
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Source Operand Types:}
@itemize
@item Numeric literals: @code{5}, @code{-42}, @code{3.14}
@item Numeric variables: @code{PIC 9(3)} (DISPLAY), @code{PIC S9(3) COMP} (BINARY)
@item Alphanumeric literals: @code{"HELLO"}, @code{'TEXT'}
@item Alphanumeric variables: @code{PIC X(20)}, @code{PIC A(10)}
@item Special registers: @code{SPACES}, @code{ZEROS}, @code{LOW-VALUES}, @code{HIGH-VALUES}
@item Function results: FUNCTION returns, computed expressions
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item BINARY unsigned (PIC 9 COMP)
8-bit or 16-bit machine integer stored in two's complement. Range 0-255 (8-bit) or 0-65535 (16-bit). MOVE converts from DISPLAY or DECIMAL to binary; fractional parts are truncated.

@item BINARY signed (PIC S9 COMP)
Signed two's complement representation. Range -128 to 127 (8-bit) or -32768 to 32767 (16-bit). Negative signs are preserved; overflow wraps around.

@item DECIMAL unsigned (PIC 9 COMP-3)
Binary-Coded Decimal (BCD) format with two digits per byte. Range 0-99999... depending on field size. Each digit occupies 4 bits; unused high nibble is zero.

@item DECIMAL signed (PIC S9 COMP-3)
BCD with explicit sign nibble. Positive values have sign nibble 0xC; negative have 0xD. Precision maintained during MOVE; sign is always preserved.

@item DISPLAY (PIC 9)
Zoned decimal: each digit is stored as an ASCII character ('0'-'9'). Sign is encoded in the units digit of the last byte (0xC for positive, 0xD for negative). MOVE converts to/from DISPLAY preserving digit values.
@end table

@strong{Type Conversion Rules:}
@itemize
@item Numeric to numeric: Magnitude preserved; fractional parts truncated; sign conversion handles negative values correctly
@item Numeric to alphanumeric: Each digit becomes a character ('0'-'9'); leading zeros retained unless using numeric edited fields
@item Alphanumeric to numeric: Only valid if source is purely numeric characters; non-numeric causes error in strict mode
@item DISPLAY to BINARY: Decimal digits converted to binary magnitude; sign preserved via magnitude
@item BINARY to DISPLAY: Binary magnitude converted to decimal digits; sign restored to units digit
@item DECIMAL to BINARY: BCD magnitude extracted and converted to binary representation; sign always preserved
@end itemize

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Numeric MOVE}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MoveExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  SOURCE-VALUE   PIC 9(3)      VALUE 123.
01  DEST-A         PIC 9(3)      VALUE 0.
01  DEST-B         PIC 9(3)      VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE SOURCE-VALUE TO DEST-A DEST-B.
    DISPLAY "DEST-A: " DEST-A.
    DISPLAY "DEST-B: " DEST-B.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
DEST-A: 123
DEST-B: 123
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Binary Type Conversion}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MoveExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DISPLAY-NUM    PIC 9(3)      VALUE 255.
01  BINARY-NUM     PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE DISPLAY-NUM TO BINARY-NUM.
    DISPLAY "Binary value stored as: " BINARY-NUM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Binary value stored as: 255
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Signed Integer with Overflow}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MoveExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  LARGE-NUM      PIC S9(4) COMP VALUE 30000.
01  SMALL-NUM      PIC S99 COMP  VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE LARGE-NUM TO SMALL-NUM.
    DISPLAY "Large value into small: " SMALL-NUM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output (with wraparound):
@example
Large value into small: 48  (30000 mod 256 = 48)
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Alphanumeric String MOVE}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MoveExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  SOURCE-STRING  PIC X(20) VALUE "HELLO WORLD".
01  DEST-STRING    PIC X(20) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE SOURCE-STRING TO DEST-STRING.
    DISPLAY "String moved: [" DEST-STRING "]".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
String moved: [HELLO WORLD         ]
@end example

@strong{Example 5: MOVE with REPLACING (String)
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MoveExample5.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  SOURCE-STR     PIC X(30) VALUE "CAT DOG CAT BIRD".
01  DEST-STR       PIC X(30) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MOVE SOURCE-STR TO DEST-STR 
        REPLACING ALL "CAT" BY "PET".
    DISPLAY "Result: " DEST-STR.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: PET DOG PET BIRD
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Overflow Behavior:}
When MOVing a value larger than the destination field can hold, Eightbol truncates from the left (most significant digits). Example: MOVing 12345 to PIC 9(3) results in 345.

@strong{Precision Loss:}
MOVing a DECIMAL (BCD) value to BINARY may lose precision if the decimal value exceeds binary capacity. Example: MOVing 99999 (BCD) to PIC 9(2) COMP results in overflow.

@strong{Sign Handling in BINARY:}
Signed binary moves preserve the sign bit; negative numbers use two's complement. MOVing -100 to PIC S9 COMP stores as 156 (two's complement of -100 in 8-bit).

@strong{Truncation of Decimals:}
MOVE always truncates fractional parts without rounding. Example: MOVing 3.99 to PIC 9(1) results in 3.

@strong{Leading Zeros in Numeric Display:}
Numeric literals without explicit leading zeros are padded with zeros when MOVed to larger DISPLAY fields. Example: MOVing 5 to PIC 9(3) results in 005.

@node ADD Statement
@section ADD Statement

The ADD statement performs arithmetic addition on numeric values, with automatic handling of BINARY, DECIMAL, and DISPLAY formats. The result can be stored in one or more destinations or directly modified in-place (GIVING clause).

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
ADD source1 source2 [source3 ...] TO destination [GIVING result]
ADD source1 source2 [source3 ...] GIVING result [TO destination ...]
ADD numeric-literal TO variable [ROUNDED]
@end example

@table @asis
@item ADD
Keyword indicating an addition operation

@item source1, source2, ...
Numeric operands to be added. Can be numeric literals, variables, or computed expressions. All sources must be numeric types (BINARY, DECIMAL, or DISPLAY)

@item TO
Keyword indicating that destination receives the sum (used with first syntax form). Modifies destination in-place.

@item destination
Variable that receives the result. When no GIVING clause is used, destination is modified in-place.

@item GIVING
Optional keyword introducing an explicit result variable. When used, destination is not modified; result receives the sum instead.

@item ROUNDED
Optional keyword for rounding. When present, fractional results are rounded to nearest integer; omitted means truncation (Eightbol default).

@item result
Variable receiving the computed sum when GIVING clause is present
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Source Operand Types:}
@itemize
@item Numeric literals: @code{5}, @code{-42}, @code{100}
@item Numeric variables (all types): BINARY signed/unsigned, DECIMAL signed/unsigned, DISPLAY
@item Computed results from nested ADDs or other arithmetic operations
@end itemize

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item BINARY Addition
Both operands converted to binary magnitude; addition performed using 6502 ADC (Add with Carry) or equivalent. Carry flag used for overflow detection. Result stored back to binary destination.

@item DECIMAL Addition
BCD digits aligned by significance; addition performed digit-by-digit with carry propagation. Each BCD digit (0-9) added; if sum exceeds 9, carry generated to next digit.

@item DISPLAY Addition
ASCII digit characters converted to numeric values; addition performed on raw values. Result re-encoded as ASCII.

@item Mixed-Type Addition
All operands automatically converted to a common type based on precision requirements. Highest precision type (usually DECIMAL) used for intermediate results; final result stored in destination format.
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Integer Addition}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AddExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE-A    PIC 9(3)   VALUE 100.
01  VALUE-B    PIC 9(3)   VALUE 50.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(4)   VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    ADD VALUE-A VALUE-B GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "100 + 50 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
100 + 50 = 0150
@end example

@strong{Example 2: In-Place Addition with TO}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AddExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  COUNTER    PIC 9(3)   VALUE 10.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    ADD 5 TO COUNTER.
    DISPLAY "Counter after ADD: " COUNTER.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Counter after ADD: 015
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Multiple Operand Addition}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AddExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  A PIC 9(2) VALUE 10.
01  B PIC 9(2) VALUE 20.
01  C PIC 9(2) VALUE 30.
01  SUM PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    ADD A B C GIVING SUM.
    DISPLAY "10 + 20 + 30 = " SUM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
10 + 20 + 30 = 060
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Binary Signed Integer Addition}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AddExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NEG-VALUE  PIC S99 COMP VALUE -50.
01  POS-VALUE  PIC S99 COMP VALUE 30.
01  RESULT     PIC S999 COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    ADD NEG-VALUE POS-VALUE GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: -20
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Overflow Detection}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AddExample5.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  MAX-8BIT   PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 255.
01  ONE        PIC 9(1) COMP VALUE 1.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    ADD MAX-8BIT ONE GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "255 + 1 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output (with wraparound):
@example
255 + 1 = 0  (wraparound in 8-bit)
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Overflow Handling:}
When the sum exceeds the destination's capacity, Eightbol wraps around (uses modulo arithmetic for BINARY). No exception is raised; the carry flag may be set in the 6502 backend.

@strong{Sign Propagation:}
Adding two negative numbers produces a negative result; adding positive and negative follows two's complement arithmetic. Example: -50 + 30 = -20.

@strong{Precision in Mixed Types:}
When adding DECIMAL and BINARY operands, the result uses DECIMAL precision for intermediate calculation; final result uses destination format.

@strong{Zero Result:}
Adding equal positive and negative values produces zero. Example: 100 + (-100) = 0. The zero flag is set in 6502 backend.

@strong{ROUNDED Clause:}
Currently not fully implemented in Eightbol COBOL; truncation is default. Future versions will support ROUNDED for fractional results.

@node SUBTRACT Statement
@section SUBTRACT Statement

The SUBTRACT statement performs arithmetic subtraction on numeric values. The minuend (first operand) is reduced by the subtrahend(s), with automatic handling of all numeric types and proper sign propagation.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
SUBTRACT source1 [source2 ...] FROM destination [GIVING result]
SUBTRACT source1 FROM destination1 [destination2 ...]
SUBTRACT source1 [source2 ...] FROM source3 GIVING result
@end example

@table @asis
@item SUBTRACT
Keyword indicating a subtraction operation

@item source1, source2, ...
Numeric operands to be subtracted. Can be numeric literals or variables.

@item FROM
Keyword separating subtrahend(s) from minuend

@item destination
Variable to receive result. When no GIVING clause, destination is modified in-place.

@item GIVING
Optional keyword for explicit result variable. When used, destination is not modified.

@item result
Variable receiving the computed difference when GIVING clause is present
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Subtraction Semantics:}
SUBTRACT calculates: destination - source1 [- source2 ...] = result

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item BINARY Subtraction
Both operands converted to binary; subtraction performed using 6502 SBC (Subtract with Carry) instruction. Borrow (inverse of carry) propagates through multi-byte operands. Result stored in BINARY format.

@item DECIMAL Subtraction
BCD operands aligned by significance; subtraction performed digit-by-digit with borrow propagation. Each digit subtracted; if result negative, borrow from next position.

@item DISPLAY Subtraction
ASCII digits converted to numeric values; subtraction performed; result re-encoded as DISPLAY.

@item Negative Results
Sign bit set appropriately for signed types (BINARY signed, DECIMAL signed). DISPLAY shows negative sign in last digit (0xD).
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Subtraction}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubtractExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  MINUEND    PIC 9(3) VALUE 100.
01  SUBTRAHEND PIC 9(3) VALUE 30.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT SUBTRAHEND FROM MINUEND GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "100 - 30 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
100 - 30 = 070
@end example

@strong{Example 2: In-Place Subtraction}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubtractExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  BALANCE    PIC 9(4) VALUE 1000.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT 250 FROM BALANCE.
    DISPLAY "Balance: " BALANCE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Balance: 0750
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Multiple Subtrahends}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubtractExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  START      PIC 9(3) VALUE 100.
01  SUB-A      PIC 9(2) VALUE 10.
01  SUB-B      PIC 9(2) VALUE 20.
01  SUB-C      PIC 9(2) VALUE 15.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT SUB-A SUB-B SUB-C FROM START GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "100 - 10 - 20 - 15 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
100 - 10 - 20 - 15 = 055
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Signed Integer Subtraction}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubtractExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE-A    PIC S99 COMP VALUE -50.
01  VALUE-B    PIC S99 COMP VALUE 30.
01  RESULT     PIC S999 COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT VALUE-B FROM VALUE-A GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: -80
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Underflow with Unsigned}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubtractExample5.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE-A    PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 50.
01  VALUE-B    PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 100.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SUBTRACT VALUE-B FROM VALUE-A GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output (wraparound):
@example
Result: 206  (wraparound: 50 - 100 = -50, stored as unsigned wraps)
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Negative Results from Unsigned:}
Subtracting a larger unsigned value from a smaller one produces wraparound. Example: 50 - 100 in 8-bit unsigned = 206 (two's complement representation).

@strong{Borrow Propagation in Multi-Byte:}
Multi-byte subtraction properly propagates borrow through all bytes. Carry flag used to track borrow in 6502.

@strong{Sign in Signed Arithmetic:}
Negative - Positive = Negative. Positive - Negative = Positive. Result sign determined by magnitude difference.

@strong{Zero Result:}
Subtracting equal values produces zero; zero flag set in 6502.

@node MULTIPLY Statement
@section MULTIPLY Statement

The MULTIPLY statement performs multiplication on numeric values. Both operands must be numeric; the product may be larger than either operand, so destination sizing is important for avoiding overflow.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
MULTIPLY source1 BY source2 GIVING result
MULTIPLY source1 BY source2 TO result [TO result2 ...]
MULTIPLY source1 BY source2 [ROUNDED]
@end example

@table @asis
@item MULTIPLY
Keyword indicating a multiplication operation

@item source1, source2
Numeric operands to be multiplied. Both must be numeric types.

@item BY
Keyword separating multiplicand from multiplier

@item GIVING
Optional keyword for explicit result variable

@item result
Variable receiving the product

@item ROUNDED
Optional keyword for rounding (not currently supported in Eightbol)
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Multiplication Semantics:}
MULTIPLY calculates: source1 × source2 = product

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item BINARY Multiplication
Both operands in binary; product computed using 6502 multiplication routine (either hardware or software). Result fits in destination with potential overflow if not sized correctly.

@item DECIMAL Multiplication
BCD operands multiplied; result BCD. Each BCD digit multiplied by each digit of second operand; partial results summed with position shifts.

@item DISPLAY Multiplication
ASCII digits converted to values; multiplication performed; result re-encoded as DISPLAY.

@item Result Size
Product of two N-digit numbers produces up to 2N digits. Example: 99 × 99 = 9801 (4 digits). Destination must be sized accordingly.
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Multiplication}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MultiplyExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  FACTOR-A   PIC 9(2) VALUE 12.
01  FACTOR-B   PIC 9(2) VALUE 15.
01  PRODUCT    PIC 9(4) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MULTIPLY FACTOR-A BY FACTOR-B GIVING PRODUCT.
    DISPLAY "12 * 15 = " PRODUCT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
12 * 15 = 0180
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Multiply by Constant}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MultiplyExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 25.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(4) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MULTIPLY VALUE BY 10 GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "25 * 10 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
25 * 10 = 0250
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Signed Multiplication}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MultiplyExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  FACTOR-A   PIC S99 COMP VALUE -10.
01  FACTOR-B   PIC S99 COMP VALUE 8.
01  RESULT     PIC S999 COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MULTIPLY FACTOR-A BY FACTOR-B GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: -80
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Overflow in Multiplication}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MultiplyExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  FACTOR-A   PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 200.
01  FACTOR-B   PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 200.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    MULTIPLY FACTOR-A BY FACTOR-B GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output (overflow):
@example
Result: 206  (200*200=40000; 40000 mod 256 = 64; but with 16-bit: 40000)
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Destination Sizing:}
Product of two N-digit numbers can produce up to 2N digits. Example: 99 × 99 requires 4 digits (9801). If destination is only 3 digits, overflow occurs.

@strong{Negative Multiplication:}
Negative × Negative = Positive. Negative × Positive = Negative. Sign determined by XOR of input signs.

@strong{Multiplication by Zero:}
Any value × 0 = 0, even for negative values or overflow-prone operands.

@strong{Multiplication by One:}
Value × 1 = Value. No side effects or precision loss.

@node DIVIDE Statement
@section DIVIDE Statement

The DIVIDE statement performs integer division on numeric values. The quotient is stored in the result variable; the remainder can optionally be captured. Division by zero causes a run-time error unless handled explicitly.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
DIVIDE source1 BY source2 GIVING quotient [REMAINDER remainder]
DIVIDE source1 BY source2 GIVING quotient TO quotient2 [...]
DIVIDE source1 BY source2 [ROUNDED]
@end example

@table @asis
@item DIVIDE
Keyword indicating a division operation

@item source1
Dividend (numerator)

@item BY
Keyword separating dividend from divisor

@item source2
Divisor (denominator). Must not be zero.

@item GIVING
Keyword introducing quotient variable

@item quotient
Variable receiving the integer quotient

@item REMAINDER
Optional keyword for capturing remainder

@item remainder
Variable receiving the remainder (source1 mod source2)

@item ROUNDED
Optional keyword for rounding (not currently supported in Eightbol)
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Division Semantics:}
DIVIDE calculates: source1 ÷ source2 = quotient (with optional remainder)

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item BINARY Division
Both operands in binary; division performed using 6502 division routine (software or hardware). Integer division truncates remainder; remainder captured if requested.

@item DECIMAL Division
BCD operands; division algorithm produces BCD quotient and remainder. Each digit compared and divided.

@item DISPLAY Division
ASCII digits converted; division performed; result re-encoded as DISPLAY.

@item Remainder
Remainder satisfies: Dividend = Quotient × Divisor + Remainder. Remainder range: 0 to Divisor-1 (for unsigned); sign follows dividend for signed.
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Division}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DivideExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DIVIDEND   PIC 9(3) VALUE 50.
01  DIVISOR    PIC 9(2) VALUE 7.
01  QUOTIENT   PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DIVIDE DIVIDEND BY DIVISOR GIVING QUOTIENT.
    DISPLAY "50 / 7 = " QUOTIENT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
50 / 7 = 007
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Division with Remainder}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DivideExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DIVIDEND   PIC 9(3) VALUE 50.
01  DIVISOR    PIC 9(2) VALUE 7.
01  QUOTIENT   PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.
01  REMAINDER  PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DIVIDE DIVIDEND BY DIVISOR GIVING QUOTIENT 
           REMAINDER REMAINDER.
    DISPLAY "50 / 7 = " QUOTIENT " remainder " REMAINDER.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
50 / 7 = 007 remainder 01
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Signed Integer Division}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DivideExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DIVIDEND   PIC S999 COMP VALUE -100.
01  DIVISOR    PIC S99 COMP VALUE 8.
01  QUOTIENT   PIC S999 COMP VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DIVIDE DIVIDEND BY DIVISOR GIVING QUOTIENT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " QUOTIENT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: -12
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Division by One}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DivideExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 42.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DIVIDE VALUE BY 1 GIVING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: 042
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Division by Zero:}
Attempting DIVIDE BY 0 causes a run-time error. Eightbol may trap this with an exception or fault message. Programmer should validate divisor before division.

@strong{Truncation:}
Integer division truncates toward zero. Example: 7 / 2 = 3 (not 3.5). Remainder is 1.

@strong{Negative Division:}
-50 / 7 = -7 (remainder -1). Sign follows dividend in Eightbol implementation.

@strong{Remainder Range:}
For unsigned: 0 ≤ remainder < divisor. For signed: sign matches dividend; magnitude < divisor.

@node COMPUTE Statement
@section COMPUTE Statement

The COMPUTE statement evaluates complex arithmetic expressions and assigns the result to a variable. It provides a more natural mathematical syntax than separate ADD/SUBTRACT/MULTIPLY/DIVIDE statements and supports operator precedence and parentheses.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
COMPUTE destination = expression
COMPUTE destination = expression1 [+ | - | * | /] expression2 [...]
COMPUTE destination = (expression) [ROUNDED]
@end example

@table @asis
@item COMPUTE
Keyword indicating an expression evaluation and assignment

@item destination
Variable to receive the computed result

@item =
Assignment operator (always required; no TO keyword for COMPUTE)

@item expression
Arithmetic expression using operands (literals, variables) and operators (+, -, *, /, **)

@item +
Addition operator (same precedence as -)

@item -
Subtraction operator (same precedence as +)

@item *
Multiplication operator (higher precedence than +/-)

@item /
Division operator (same precedence as *)

@item **
Exponentiation operator (highest precedence)

@item (expression)
Parenthesized sub-expression; forces evaluation order

@item ROUNDED
Optional keyword for rounding (not currently supported in Eightbol)
@end table

@subsection Operand Types and Conversions

@strong{Expression Evaluation:}
COMPUTE evaluates expressions left-to-right within precedence rules. Operator precedence (highest to lowest): @strong{**}, @strong{* /}, @strong{+ -}. Parentheses override precedence.

@strong{Numeric Type Handling:}

@table @asis
@item Intermediate Results
All intermediate calculations use highest precision available among operands. DECIMAL used if any operand is DECIMAL; otherwise BINARY.

@item Truncation
Division results truncate toward zero; no rounding unless ROUNDED clause used (not supported in current Eightbol).

@item Final Result
Result stored in destination format; if precision lost, truncation or overflow may occur.

@item Sign Propagation
Negative values propagate correctly through all operations; result sign determined by operations.
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Arithmetic Expression}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ComputeExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  A          PIC 9(2) VALUE 10.
01  B          PIC 9(2) VALUE 5.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    COMPUTE RESULT = A + B * 2.
    DISPLAY "10 + 5 * 2 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
10 + 5 * 2 = 020  (5*2=10, 10+10=20)
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Complex Expression with Parentheses}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ComputeExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  X          PIC 9(2) VALUE 3.
01  Y          PIC 9(2) VALUE 4.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    COMPUTE RESULT = (X + Y) * 2.
    DISPLAY "(3 + 4) * 2 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
(3 + 4) * 2 = 014
@end example

@strong{Example 3: Division and Truncation}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ComputeExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NUMERATOR   PIC 9(2) VALUE 50.
01  DENOMINATOR PIC 9(2) VALUE 7.
01  RESULT      PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    COMPUTE RESULT = NUMERATOR / DENOMINATOR.
    DISPLAY "50 / 7 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
50 / 7 = 07
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Nested Expressions}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ComputeExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  A          PIC 9(2) VALUE 2.
01  B          PIC 9(2) VALUE 3.
01  C          PIC 9(2) VALUE 4.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    COMPUTE RESULT = A * (B + C).
    DISPLAY "2 * (3 + 4) = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
2 * (3 + 4) = 014
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Exponentiation}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ComputeExample5.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  BASE       PIC 9(2) VALUE 2.
01  EXPONENT   PIC 9(1) VALUE 8.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    COMPUTE RESULT = BASE ** EXPONENT.
    DISPLAY "2 ** 8 = " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
2 ** 8 = 256  (wraps to overflow if PIC 9(3))
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Operator Precedence:}
Exponentiation (**) has highest precedence, followed by multiplication/division, then addition/subtraction. Parentheses override precedence: (2 + 3) * 4 = 20, but 2 + 3 * 4 = 14.

@strong{Division Truncation:}
COMPUTE always truncates division results toward zero. Example: 10 / 3 = 3 (not 3.33 or 4).

@strong{Intermediate Overflow:}
If an intermediate result exceeds destination capacity, overflow may occur. Example: COMPUTE PIC 9(2) = 10 * 100 produces 0 (40000 mod 256 or wrapped).

@strong{Negative Numbers:}
COMPUTE handles negative numbers correctly through all operations. Example: COMPUTE X = -10 + 3 produces -7.

@node IF/THEN/ELSE Statements
@section IF/THEN/ELSE Statements

The IF statement provides conditional execution of code blocks based on boolean conditions. Conditions can be numeric comparisons, string comparisons, or logical combinations using AND/OR/NOT operators. Nested IF blocks are allowed.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
IF condition
    statement-block
[ELSE
    statement-block]
END-IF

IF condition1 AND/OR condition2
    statement-block
END-IF

IF x [IS] GREATER|LESS|EQUAL [TO] y
    statement-block
ELSE
    other-block
END-IF
@end example

@table @asis
@item IF
Keyword starting a conditional statement

@item condition
Boolean expression to evaluate. Can use comparison operators (>, <, =, >=, <=, /=) or logical operators (AND, OR, NOT).

@item IS
Optional keyword in condition (e.g., "x IS GREATER THAN y" or "x > y")

@item GREATER, LESS, EQUAL, NOT EQUAL
Comparison operator keywords (alternatives to >, <, =, /=)

@item TO
Optional keyword in condition (e.g., "x > y" or "x GREATER TO y")

@item THEN
Optional keyword after condition; typically omitted in modern COBOL

@item statement-block
One or more executable statements within the IF block

@item ELSE
Optional keyword introducing alternative code path if condition is false

@item END-IF
Keyword terminating the IF statement (required in Eightbol)
@end table

@subsection Condition Types and Evaluation

@strong{Numeric Comparison:}
@itemize
@item x = y (equal)
@item x > y (greater than)
@item x < y (less than)
@item x >= y (greater than or equal)
@item x <= y (less than or equal)
@item x /= y or x NOT= y (not equal)
@end itemize

@strong{Alphanumeric Comparison:}
String comparison uses ASCII order. Strings are compared character-by-character.

@strong{Logical Operators:}
@itemize
@item condition1 AND condition2 (both must be true)
@item condition1 OR condition2 (either can be true)
@item NOT condition (negates result)
@end itemize

@strong{Type Coercion:}
Numeric and alphanumeric types are compared appropriately. Numeric comparison uses magnitude; alphanumeric uses ASCII collation.

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple IF/ELSE}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. IfExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  AGE        PIC 9(2) VALUE 25.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF AGE >= 18
        DISPLAY "Adult"
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Minor"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Adult
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Nested IF Statements}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. IfExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  SCORE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 85.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF SCORE >= 90
        DISPLAY "Grade: A"
    ELSE
        IF SCORE >= 80
            DISPLAY "Grade: B"
        ELSE
            DISPLAY "Grade: C or lower"
        END-IF
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Grade: B
@end example

@strong{Example 3: AND Condition}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. IfExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  AGE        PIC 9(2) VALUE 25.
01  LICENSE    PIC X(1) VALUE "Y".

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF AGE >= 18 AND LICENSE = "Y"
        DISPLAY "Can drive"
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Cannot drive"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Can drive
@end example

@strong{Example 4: String Comparison}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. IfExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NAME       PIC X(10) VALUE "Alice".

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF NAME = "Alice"
        DISPLAY "Hello, Alice!"
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Hello, stranger!"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Hello, Alice!
@end example

@strong{Example 5: Compound Condition with OR}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. IfExample5.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  FRUIT      PIC X(6) VALUE "Apple".

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF FRUIT = "Apple" OR FRUIT = "Orange"
        DISPLAY "Citrus or Apple"
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Other fruit"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Citrus or Apple
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{String Comparison with Trailing Spaces:}
"Hello" compared with "Hello   " (padded) may not be equal if exact comparison is required. Trailing spaces matter in PIC X fields.

@strong{Numeric String Comparison:}
Comparing "10" with "2" as strings yields "10" < "2" (ASCII order). Compare as numbers for arithmetic order: 10 > 2.

@strong{Negative Numbers:}
Comparisons with negative numbers work correctly: -50 < -10 < 0 < 10. Sign is properly considered.

@strong{Floating-Point Not Supported:}
Eightbol COBOL does not support floating-point; all comparisons use integer or BCD arithmetic.

@node EVALUATE Statement
@section EVALUATE Statement

The EVALUATE statement (also known as CASE in other languages) provides a more concise way to handle multiple conditions compared to nested IF/ELSE blocks. A value is evaluated against multiple WHEN clauses, and the first matching clause executes.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
EVALUATE expression
    WHEN value1
        statement-block
    WHEN value2
        statement-block
    WHEN OTHER
        statement-block
END-EVALUATE

EVALUATE TRUE
    WHEN condition1
        statement-block
    WHEN condition2
        statement-block
END-EVALUATE
@end example

@table @asis
@item EVALUATE
Keyword starting an evaluation statement

@item expression
Value to be evaluated against WHEN clauses

@item WHEN
Keyword introducing a comparison value or condition

@item value1, value2
Literal or computed values to compare against expression

@item TRUE
Special keyword for condition-based EVALUATE (used instead of a value)

@item condition1, condition2
Boolean conditions evaluated sequentially

@item WHEN OTHER
Default clause if no WHEN matches

@item END-EVALUATE
Keyword terminating the EVALUATE statement
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple EVALUATE with Constants}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. EvaluateExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DAY-CODE   PIC 9(1) VALUE 3.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    EVALUATE DAY-CODE
        WHEN 1
            DISPLAY "Monday"
        WHEN 2
            DISPLAY "Tuesday"
        WHEN 3
            DISPLAY "Wednesday"
        WHEN OTHER
            DISPLAY "Other day"
    END-EVALUATE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Wednesday
@end example

@strong{Example 2: EVALUATE with TRUE for Conditions}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. EvaluateExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  SCORE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 75.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    EVALUATE TRUE
        WHEN SCORE >= 90
            DISPLAY "Excellent"
        WHEN SCORE >= 80
            DISPLAY "Good"
        WHEN SCORE >= 70
            DISPLAY "Fair"
        WHEN OTHER
            DISPLAY "Poor"
    END-EVALUATE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Fair
@end example

@strong{Example 3: String Matching}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. EvaluateExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  COMMAND    PIC X(4) VALUE "QUIT".

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    EVALUATE COMMAND
        WHEN "PLAY"
            DISPLAY "Starting game"
        WHEN "QUIT"
            DISPLAY "Exiting"
        WHEN "HELP"
            DISPLAY "Help menu"
        WHEN OTHER
            DISPLAY "Unknown command"
    END-EVALUATE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Exiting
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Fall-Through Not Allowed:}
Unlike C switch statements, Eightbol EVALUATE does not fall through. Only one WHEN block executes.

@strong{Multiple Matching WHEN:}
If multiple WHEN clauses match, only the first is executed. Other WEHNs are skipped.

@strong{WHEN OTHER Required:}
While not syntactically required, it is good practice to include WHEN OTHER for unmatched cases.

@node PERFORM Statement
@section PERFORM Statement

The PERFORM statement calls a procedure or repeats a code block multiple times. It can perform a named procedure, a paragraph, or a block of inline code. PERFORM supports optional looping with UNTIL or TIMES modifiers.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
PERFORM procedure-name
PERFORM UNTIL condition
    statement-block
END-PERFORM

PERFORM procedure-name UNTIL condition

PERFORM procedure-name counter TIMES

PERFORM paragraph-name UNTIL counter > 10
@end example

@table @asis
@item PERFORM
Keyword starting a procedure call or loop

@item procedure-name
Name of a defined procedure or paragraph to call

@item UNTIL
Keyword for conditional loop termination. Block repeats until condition becomes true.

@item condition
Boolean condition evaluated before each iteration

@item statement-block
Inline code to repeat

@item END-PERFORM
Keyword terminating an inline PERFORM block

@item counter
Numeric variable specifying iteration count for TIMES clause

@item TIMES
Keyword indicating fixed iteration count
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Procedure Call}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. PerformExample1.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM PRINT-MESSAGE.
    STOP RUN.

PRINT-MESSAGE.
    DISPLAY "Hello from procedure".
@end example

Output:
@example
Hello from procedure
@end example

@strong{Example 2: PERFORM UNTIL Loop}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. PerformExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  COUNTER    PIC 9(2) VALUE 1.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM UNTIL COUNTER > 5
        DISPLAY "Count: " COUNTER
        ADD 1 TO COUNTER
    END-PERFORM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Count: 1
Count: 2
Count: 3
Count: 4
Count: 5
@end example

@strong{Example 3: PERFORM TIMES}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. PerformExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  ITERATIONS PIC 9(1) VALUE 3.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM ITERATIONS TIMES
        DISPLAY "Iteration"
    END-PERFORM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Iteration
Iteration
Iteration
@end example

@strong{Example 4: Procedure with UNTIL}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. PerformExample4.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 1.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM GENERATE-POWERS UNTIL VALUE > 100.
    STOP RUN.

GENERATE-POWERS.
    DISPLAY "Power: " VALUE.
    MULTIPLY VALUE BY 2 GIVING VALUE.
@end example

Output:
@example
Power: 1
Power: 2
Power: 4
Power: 8
Power: 16
Power: 32
Power: 64
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Infinite Loops:}
If UNTIL condition never becomes true, PERFORM loops infinitely. Programmer must ensure condition is eventually satisfied.

@strong{Procedure Return:}
PERFORM automatically returns control after procedure completes. No explicit return needed.

@strong{TIMES with Zero:}
PERFORM 0 TIMES executes the block zero times (block is skipped).

@node CALL and INVOKE Statements
@section CALL and INVOKE Statements

The CALL statement invokes an external subroutine or library function, passing parameters and receiving return values. The INVOKE statement is used for object-oriented method calls. Both statements handle parameter passing and type conversions.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
CALL "program-name" USING parameter1 parameter2 ...
    RETURNING result

CALL program-name USING BY REFERENCE var1
                       BY VALUE const1
    RETURNING result

INVOKE object "method-name" USING parameter1
    RETURNING result
@end example

@table @asis
@item CALL
Keyword invoking an external program or subroutine

@item program-name
Name of the program to call (as string or variable)

@item USING
Keyword introducing parameter list

@item BY REFERENCE
Parameters passed by reference (modifications affect original variable)

@item BY VALUE
Parameters passed by value (copy passed; original unaffected)

@item parameter1, parameter2
Variables or literals to pass to the called program

@item RETURNING
Keyword introducing return value

@item result
Variable to receive return value from called program

@item INVOKE
Keyword for object-oriented method calls

@item object
Object reference (instance) on which to invoke method

@item method-name
Name of the method to invoke (as string)
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple CALL}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. CallExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  RESULT     PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    CALL "AddTen" USING RESULT RETURNING RESULT.
    DISPLAY "Result: " RESULT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: CALL with Multiple Parameters}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. CallExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  A          PIC 9(2) VALUE 5.
01  B          PIC 9(2) VALUE 3.
01  SUM        PIC 9(3) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    CALL "Add" USING A B SUM RETURNING SUM.
    DISPLAY "Sum: " SUM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@strong{Example 3: BY VALUE vs BY REFERENCE}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. CallExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(2) VALUE 10.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    CALL "ModifyValue" USING BY REFERENCE VALUE.
    DISPLAY "Modified: " VALUE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Program Not Found:}
If the called program cannot be found, CALL raises an error at run-time.

@strong{Parameter Mismatch:}
If the number or types of parameters don't match, results are undefined or error occurs.

@strong{RETURNING without Value:}
If the called program doesn't return a value but RETURNING is specified, result contains garbage or zero.

@node STRING Statement
@section STRING Statement

The STRING statement concatenates multiple string and numeric values into a single output string. It provides flexible string concatenation with optional delimiters and pointer management.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
STRING source1 DELIMITED BY delimiter1
       source2 DELIMITED BY delimiter2
    INTO destination
    [WITH POINTER pointer-var]

STRING "Hello" DELIMITED BY SIZE
       " " DELIMITED BY SIZE
       "World" DELIMITED BY SIZE
    INTO output-string
    END-STRING
@end example

@table @asis
@item STRING
Keyword starting a string concatenation operation

@item source1, source2
String or numeric values to concatenate

@item DELIMITED BY
Keyword specifying when source contribution ends

@item delimiter1, delimiter2
Character or SIZE keyword defining stop point. SIZE means entire string; other values are terminating characters.

@item INTO
Keyword introducing destination string

@item destination
Target string variable to receive concatenation result

@item WITH POINTER
Optional clause for managing position within destination

@item pointer-var
Numeric variable tracking position in destination string. Automatically advanced; can be pre-set to skip positions.

@item END-STRING
Keyword terminating STRING statement (optional but recommended)
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple String Concatenation}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. StringExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  GREETING   PIC X(30) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    STRING "Hello" DELIMITED BY SIZE
           " " DELIMITED BY SIZE
           "World" DELIMITED BY SIZE
        INTO GREETING
    END-STRING.
    DISPLAY GREETING.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Hello World                 
@end example

@strong{Example 2: STRING with DELIMITED BY CHARACTER}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. StringExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  TEXT       PIC X(20) VALUE "Hello-World-Test".
01  RESULT     PIC X(30) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    STRING TEXT DELIMITED BY "-"
        INTO RESULT
    END-STRING.
    DISPLAY "Result: [" RESULT "]".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Result: [Hello                       ]
@end example

@strong{Example 3: STRING with Numeric Conversion}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. StringExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NAME       PIC X(10) VALUE "Player".
01  SCORE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 100.
01  MESSAGE    PIC X(40) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    STRING "Name: " DELIMITED BY SIZE
           NAME DELIMITED BY SIZE
           " Score: " DELIMITED BY SIZE
           SCORE DELIMITED BY SIZE
        INTO MESSAGE
    END-STRING.
    DISPLAY MESSAGE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Name: Player Score: 100
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Insufficient Destination Space:}
If concatenation exceeds destination size, Eightbol truncates the result. No error is raised.

@strong{DELIMITED BY Character Not Found:}
If delimiter is not found, entire source is included (equivalent to DELIMITED BY SIZE).

@strong{POINTER Overflow:}
If POINTER exceeds destination length, result is undefined or truncated.

@node UNSTRING Statement
@section UNSTRING Statement

The UNSTRING statement splits a source string into multiple substrings using delimiters, effectively reversing STRING concatenation. It allows flexible parsing of formatted text with various delimiters.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
UNSTRING source DELIMITED BY delimiter1
    INTO destination1
         destination2
    [WITH POINTER pointer-var]
    END-UNSTRING

UNSTRING line DELIMITED BY ":"
    INTO field1 field2 field3
    END-UNSTRING
@end example

@table @asis
@item UNSTRING
Keyword starting string splitting operation

@item source
Source string to be parsed

@item DELIMITED BY
Keyword specifying delimiter(s)

@item delimiter1
Character(s) used as field separator(s). Multiple delimiters can be listed with OR.

@item INTO
Keyword introducing destination list

@item destination1, destination2
Variables to receive parsed substrings

@item WITH POINTER
Optional clause for managing position within source string

@item pointer-var
Numeric variable tracking position. Automatically advanced after each field extraction.

@item END-UNSTRING
Keyword terminating UNSTRING statement
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple UNSTRING}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. UnstringExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  INPUT      PIC X(20) VALUE "John,25,New York".
01  NAME       PIC X(10) VALUE SPACES.
01  AGE        PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.
01  CITY       PIC X(10) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    UNSTRING INPUT DELIMITED BY ","
        INTO NAME AGE CITY
    END-UNSTRING.
    DISPLAY "Name: " NAME.
    DISPLAY "Age: " AGE.
    DISPLAY "City: " CITY.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Name: John
Age: 25
City: New York
@end example

@strong{Example 2: UNSTRING with Multiple Delimiters}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. UnstringExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  PATH       PIC X(30) VALUE "dir1/dir2\\dir3".
01  PART1      PIC X(10) VALUE SPACES.
01  PART2      PIC X(10) VALUE SPACES.
01  PART3      PIC X(10) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    UNSTRING PATH DELIMITED BY "/" OR "\\"
        INTO PART1 PART2 PART3
    END-UNSTRING.
    DISPLAY "Parts: " PART1 " " PART2 " " PART3.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Fewer Destinations than Fields:}
Extra fields in source are discarded.

@strong{More Destinations than Fields:}
Extra destinations remain unchanged from their initial values.

@strong{Empty Fields:}
Consecutive delimiters create empty fields; destinations remain unchanged.

@node DISPLAY Statement
@section DISPLAY Statement

The DISPLAY statement outputs text to standard output (console). It can output literals, variables, or concatenated values with automatic type conversion.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
DISPLAY literal-or-variable [literal-or-variable] ...
DISPLAY "Text" VARIABLE-NAME "More text"
DISPLAY variable UPON CONSOLE
DISPLAY variable WITH NO ADVANCING
@end example

@table @asis
@item DISPLAY
Keyword for output operation

@item literal-or-variable
String literal (quoted), numeric literal, or variable to output. Numeric values are converted to DISPLAY format (ASCII digits).

@item UPON
Optional keyword specifying output destination

@item CONSOLE
Output device (typically STDOUT)

@item WITH NO ADVANCING
Optional keyword preventing newline at end of output

@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple DISPLAY}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DisplayExample1.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Hello, World!".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Hello, World!
@end example

@strong{Example 2: DISPLAY with Variables}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DisplayExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NAME       PIC X(10) VALUE "Alice".
01  AGE        PIC 9(2) VALUE 30.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Name: " NAME ", Age: " AGE.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Name: Alice, Age: 30
@end example

@strong{Example 3: DISPLAY with NO ADVANCING}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DisplayExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  COUNTER    PIC 9(1) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM 5 TIMES
        ADD 1 TO COUNTER
        DISPLAY COUNTER WITH NO ADVANCING
        DISPLAY " " WITH NO ADVANCING
    END-PERFORM.
    DISPLAY "".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
1 2 3 4 5 
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Numeric Conversion:}
Numeric variables automatically converted to DISPLAY format (ASCII digits). Leading zeros shown.

@strong{String Truncation:}
If output line exceeds buffer, truncation may occur silently.

@node ACCEPT Statement
@section ACCEPT Statement

The ACCEPT statement reads input from keyboard or input stream into a variable. It supports numeric and alphanumeric input with automatic type conversion.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
ACCEPT variable [FROM KEYBOARD]
ACCEPT variable [FROM CONSOLE]
ACCEPT variable [FROM LINE]
@end example

@table @asis
@item ACCEPT
Keyword for input operation

@item variable
Destination variable to receive input

@item FROM
Optional keyword specifying input source

@item KEYBOARD, CONSOLE, LINE
Input source specification (typically KEYBOARD or CONSOLE)
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple ACCEPT}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AcceptExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  NAME       PIC X(20) VALUE SPACES.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Enter your name: " WITH NO ADVANCING.
    ACCEPT NAME.
    DISPLAY "Hello, " NAME "!".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: ACCEPT Numeric Input}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. AcceptExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  AGE        PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Enter your age: " WITH NO ADVANCING.
    ACCEPT AGE.
    IF AGE >= 18
        DISPLAY "Adult"
    ELSE
        DISPLAY "Minor"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Non-Numeric Input to Numeric Field:}
Behavior is undefined; Eightbol may reject input or pad with zeros.

@strong{Input Exceeds Field Size:}
Input is truncated to fit field width.

@node GOBACK and STOP RUN Statements
@section GOBACK and STOP RUN Statements

The GOBACK statement returns control from a called program to its caller, with optional return code. The STOP RUN statement terminates the entire program, optionally returning an exit code to the operating system.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
GOBACK [RETURNING return-code]
STOP RUN [RETURNING return-code]
STOP RUN [WITH ERROR]
@end example

@table @asis
@item GOBACK
Keyword returning control to calling program

@item STOP RUN
Keyword terminating entire program execution

@item RETURNING
Optional keyword for return code

@item return-code
Numeric value (typically 0-255) returned to caller or OS

@item WITH ERROR
Optional specification indicating error status
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple GOBACK}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubroutineExample.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Subroutine executing".
    GOBACK.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: GOBACK with Return Code}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SubroutineExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  STATUS     PIC 9(1) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF success
        MOVE 0 TO STATUS
    ELSE
        MOVE 1 TO STATUS
    END-IF.
    GOBACK RETURNING STATUS.
@end example

@strong{Example 3: STOP RUN}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. MainProgram.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DISPLAY "Program complete".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{GOBACK Without Caller:}
If GOBACK is called from main program (not a subroutine), behavior is equivalent to STOP RUN.

@strong{STOP RUN Abruptness:}
STOP RUN terminates immediately; no cleanup of open files or resources occurs automatically.

@node EXIT Statement
@section EXIT Statement

The EXIT statement immediately terminates a procedure or loop, transferring control to the next statement after the enclosing block.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
EXIT
EXIT PERFORM
EXIT PARAGRAPH
EXIT METHOD
@end example

@table @asis
@item EXIT
Keyword for early termination

@item PERFORM
Exits enclosing PERFORM loop

@item PARAGRAPH
Exits current paragraph (not common in modern COBOL)

@item METHOD
Exits current method (object-oriented COBOL)
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: EXIT PERFORM}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. ExitExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  I          PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM UNTIL I > 10
        ADD 1 TO I
        IF I = 5
            EXIT PERFORM
        END-IF
        DISPLAY I
    END-PERFORM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
1
2
3
4
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{EXIT Without Context:}
EXIT outside PERFORM or METHOD is an error.

@strong{Nested EXIT:}
EXIT PERFORM exits only the immediately enclosing PERFORM, not outer loops.

@node GO TO Statement
@section GO TO Statement

The GO TO statement (GOTO) transfers control unconditionally to a labeled paragraph or section. It should be used sparingly as it can make code flow difficult to follow (considered harmful in most modern programming).

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
GO TO paragraph-name
GOTO section-name
GO TO paragraph-name DEPENDING ON variable
@end example

@table @asis
@item GO TO, GOTO
Keywords for unconditional branch

@item paragraph-name
Target paragraph to branch to

@item section-name
Target section to branch to

@item DEPENDING ON
Conditional GO TO based on variable value

@item variable
Integer variable determining which paragraph to jump to (first value jumps to first paragraph, etc.)
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple GO TO}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. GotoExample1.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    GO TO SKIP-SECTION.
    DISPLAY "This is skipped".
SKIP-SECTION.
    DISPLAY "Jumped here".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Jumped here
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Unstructured Jumps:}
GO TO can create tangled code flow and infinite loops. Avoid using; use IF/PERFORM/EVALUATE instead.

@strong{DEPENDING ON:}
GO TO DEPENDING ON is similar to C switch statement with fall-through. Index exceeding paragraph count may cause error.

@node SET Statement
@section SET Statement

The SET statement assigns values to variables, particularly useful for setting flags, boolean values, or copy register data. It is the primary method for assigning boolean TRUE/FALSE values.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
SET variable TO value
SET variable1 variable2 TO value
SET variable TO TRUE | FALSE
SET index-variable TO 1 | 2 | 3
@end example

@table @asis
@item SET
Keyword for variable assignment (especially boolean/index values)

@item variable, variable1, variable2
Target variables (can be multiple to same value)

@item TO
Keyword separating variable from value

@item value
Value to assign (numeric, TRUE/FALSE, or variable)

@item TRUE, FALSE
Boolean literal keywords
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: SET Boolean Flag}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SetExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  FLAG       PIC 9(1) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SET FLAG TO TRUE.
    IF FLAG = TRUE
        DISPLAY "Flag is set"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Flag is set
@end example

@strong{Example 2: SET Multiple Variables}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. SetExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  A          PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.
01  B          PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.
01  C          PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    SET A B C TO 10.
    DISPLAY "A=" A ", B=" B ", C=" C.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
A=10, B=10, C=10
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Boolean Interpretation:}
SET FLAG TO TRUE sets FLAG to 1; FALSE sets to 0. Any non-zero value is logically TRUE; 0 is FALSE.

@node INSPECT Statement
@section INSPECT Statement

The INSPECT statement analyzes and modifies strings by counting occurrences of characters or replacing characters. It provides string scanning and replacement without full string reconstruction.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
INSPECT string TALLYING counter FOR ALL character
INSPECT string TALLYING counter FOR LEADING character
INSPECT string REPLACING ALL char1 BY char2
INSPECT string REPLACING LEADING char1 BY char2
INSPECT string REPLACING FIRST char1 BY char2
@end example

@table @asis
@item INSPECT
Keyword for string analysis

@item string
Source string to inspect

@item TALLYING
Keyword indicating counting operation

@item counter
Numeric variable to receive count

@item FOR
Keyword preceding search specifier

@item ALL, LEADING, TRAILING, FIRST
Scope of inspection: ALL matches all occurrences, LEADING matches from start, TRAILING matches from end, FIRST matches only first occurrence

@item character, char1, char2
Characters to match and (optionally) replace

@item REPLACING
Keyword indicating replacement operation

@item BY
Keyword preceding replacement character
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: TALLYING ALL Occurrences}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. InspectExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  TEXT       PIC X(30) VALUE "HELLO WORLD HELLO AGAIN".
01  COUNT      PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    INSPECT TEXT TALLYING COUNT FOR ALL "L".
    DISPLAY "Count of L: " COUNT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Count of L: 3
@end example

@strong{Example 2: REPLACING ALL Occurrences}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. InspectExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  TEXT       PIC X(30) VALUE "CAT DOG CAT BIRD".

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    INSPECT TEXT REPLACING ALL "CAT" BY "PET".
    DISPLAY TEXT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
PET DOG PET BIRD         
@end example

@strong{Example 3: TALLYING LEADING Spaces}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. InspectExample3.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  TEXT       PIC X(20) VALUE "    HELLO".
01  COUNT      PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    INSPECT TEXT TALLYING COUNT FOR LEADING SPACE.
    DISPLAY "Leading spaces: " COUNT.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

Output:
@example
Leading spaces: 4
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Character Not Found:}
If character doesn't exist, count remains 0 or no replacement occurs.

@strong{Overlapping Matches:}
INSPECT treats matches as non-overlapping. For example, tallying "AA" in "AAA" counts as 1 (not 2).

@strong{Single Character Limitation:}
Traditional INSPECT works with single characters; substring matching may not be supported in Eightbol.

@node LOG-FAULT Statement
@section LOG-FAULT Statement

The LOG-FAULT statement writes diagnostic fault information to the system log or debug output. It is used for run-time error reporting and diagnostics.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
LOG-FAULT "fault message" [WITH variable1 variable2 ...]
LOG-FAULT code severity message-string
@end example

@table @asis
@item LOG-FAULT
Keyword for logging fault/error information

@item fault message
String literal describing the fault

@item WITH
Optional keyword for additional context variables

@item variable1, variable2
Variables to include in fault log for debugging

@item code
Numeric fault code

@item severity
Severity level (INFORMATIONAL, WARNING, ERROR, FATAL, etc.)

@item message-string
Descriptive fault message
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Fault Log}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. LogFaultExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(2) VALUE 150.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF VALUE > 100
        LOG-FAULT "Value exceeds threshold"
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Fault with Context}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. LogFaultExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  DIVISOR    PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.
01  DIVIDEND   PIC 9(3) VALUE 100.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    IF DIVISOR = 0
        LOG-FAULT "Division by zero" WITH DIVIDEND DIVISOR
    END-IF.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{Log Destination:}
LOG-FAULT output may go to STDERR, system log, or debug console depending on Eightbol environment.

@strong{Performance Impact:}
Excessive LOG-FAULT calls may slow program execution; use judiciously in loops.

@node DEBUG-BREAK Statement
@section DEBUG-BREAK Statement

The DEBUG-BREAK statement triggers a debugger breakpoint or halts execution for debugging purposes. It is useful during development for inspecting program state.

@subsection Syntax and Keywords

@example
DEBUG-BREAK
DEBUG-BREAK "breakpoint label"
DEBUG-BREAK WITH variable1 variable2
@end example

@table @asis
@item DEBUG-BREAK
Keyword triggering a breakpoint

@item breakpoint label
Optional string label for the breakpoint

@item WITH
Optional keyword for context variables
@end table

@subsection Examples

@strong{Example 1: Simple Breakpoint}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DebugExample1.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  VALUE      PIC 9(3) VALUE 50.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    DEBUG-BREAK.
    DISPLAY "After breakpoint".
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@strong{Example 2: Conditional Breakpoint}
@example
IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
PROGRAM-ID. DebugExample2.

DATA DIVISION.
WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
01  I          PIC 9(2) VALUE 0.

PROCEDURE DIVISION.
    PERFORM 10 TIMES
        ADD 1 TO I
        IF I = 5
            DEBUG-BREAK "Middle of loop"
        END-IF
    END-PERFORM.
    STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection Edge Cases

@strong{No Debugger Attached:}
If no debugger is attached, DEBUG-BREAK may be a no-op or cause program halt with error.

@strong{Production Builds:}
DEBUG-BREAK statements should be removed or disabled in production code.

@c End of COBOL Statements Comprehensive Reference
